# V11 bounded multi-session continuity (2026-10-21)

Machine: Ryzen 7 260, 16 GB RAM, RTX 5050 Laptop 8 GB at 40 W; Qwen3.5-4B Q8 CUDA, `marin serve --ctx 8192 --temp 0`.

## Audit of the server before the change (crates/cli/src/server)
- `Store` is `Mutex<Option<Stored>>`: ONE idle session. `Stored` = id + GPU cache (`EngineCache`) + `Retained` token record.
- A request with a session id TAKES the session out of the slot for its whole run (a second request for the same id
  finds nothing: 404 "session not found, evicted or in use"; "in use" and "evicted" are the same answer).
- A request with no id, or with id "new", calls `store.evict()` first: any other client's idle session is dropped
  (GPU memory freed) before the new request runs. So client B's first request kills client A's session.
- After a good reply `store.put` replaces the slot (drops the previous occupant, whoever's it was).
- Failed/cancelled/disconnected existing sessions go through `Store::recover`: cache cleared, previous tokens read
  again (a replay), and put back; a new session leaves nothing behind.
- IDs: 128 random bits from /dev/urandom, 32 hex chars, minted by the server for "new"; any well-formed id is accepted
  on input but only ids the store holds are served. Revision: the server keeps the token record (`Retained`), a request
  reuses the cache only if the record equals the cache length and is a strict prefix of the new prompt (`can_reuse`),
  otherwise the cache is rebuilt (the client's full message history is always in the request).
- The client always re-sends its full history, so a lost session costs a re-read of the history (and a 404 the client
  must handle: marin code retries once with a new session), never lost conversation text.
- Existing mechanisms to reuse: `Engine::export_state_delta/export_state_page/begin_state_import/import_state_*/
  finish_state_import/kept_tokens/state_layout` (used by checkpoint files; bytes are exact f32), `Retained::restored`,
  `Scheduled::forward` (gate, memory retry), `Store::recover`, `Gate` admission.

## Invariants
I1 a cache is owned by exactly one place at a time: a running request, the hot slot, or the cold store (as bytes).
   Cold bytes are never shared; a restore builds a fresh cache.
I2 an id is served only if the store holds it (hot or cold) and no request is running it; otherwise 404 (unknown) or
   409 (running). A session that was displaced is still known until the cold budget drops it.
I3 a resumed cache holds exactly the tokens it held when it was demoted; the first request after resume reuses it only
   under the same `can_reuse` rule as a hot session. Greedy output after resume equals output without demotion.
I4 failed, cancelled or timed-out requests leave the session as it was before them (as today).
I5 host memory held by cold sessions never exceeds the budget; sessions are dropped oldest first; a session that alone
   exceeds the budget is not kept. No server conversation is written to disk.
I6 GPU memory of idle sessions never exceeds what is held today (one hot session).
I7 a restore that cannot get GPU memory is refused 503 and the cold session stays.

## Gates (before implementation)
A  continuity: A->B->A->B... with two clients: today every return of A is a 404 (or a replay); after: 0 x 404 and
   reused > 0, replayed == 0 on every return within the budget.
B  cold resume cost <= 25% of a full re-read of the same history (re-read: 7.7 s per 1k tokens).
C  single client: p50 total latency and decode tok/s within 2% of the current server; peak VRAM within +128 MiB.
D  2-3 concurrent clients: aggregate throughput within 5% of the current server; no 5xx.
E  greedy outputs identical with and without forced demotion, across cancel, disconnect, failure and restart-free runs.
F  bounds: cold host bytes <= budget; session count <= cap; peak RSS grows by at most budget + 256 MiB; overload and
   oversized sessions answer predictably (404/503), never hang.
G  tests: state transitions (demote, restore, drop, contention, failed restore, cancel) fail if broken.
GO only if A, B, C, D, E, F, G hold. If a cold session cannot be bounded, stop with an alternative.

## Design built (smallest change that meets the gates)
Hot/cold, two tiers only. One session stays hot (cache on the GPU, as before). A session that is displaced (another
session finishes, or a request starts a new one) is copied to host memory (`server/cold.rs`: tokens, each DeltaNet
layer block, each KV page, via the existing checkpoint export/import calls; exact f32 bytes) and its GPU cache is
freed. A request for a cold id thaws it into a new cache after the hot session makes room. Limits (constants):
16 cold sessions, 2 GiB together, and 1 GiB of host memory must stay available after a copy; the least recently used
goes first; a session bigger than the budget is not kept. `Store` (server/session.rs) now holds the hot slot, the cold
queue and the claimed ids; `claim` takes a session out of the store and returns a `Lease` (dropping it frees the id), so a
running session is in no list: a second request for it gets 409, a dropped/unknown/restart-lost id gets 404, and a
displacement can never touch a running cache. Only Qwen3.5 on CUDA can shelve (the engines that cannot behave as before).
A failed thaw for want of GPU memory puts the cold session back and answers 503 (retryable); other failures drop it.
Changed: server/{cold,session,sched,completion,http,mod}.rs; tests: 8 store unit tests, tests/server_cold.rs (GPU),
tests/common/server.rs (shared by server_pressure.rs).

## Results (Qwen3.5-4B Q8 CUDA, --ctx 8192, greedy, 24-token replies, release builds, same machine, 40 W)
| Scenario | Before | After |
|---|---|---|
| A,B,A,B... 4 rounds, ~1.1k-token histories | 6 of 8 turns 404; every return re-read the history: 8.3-8.9 s to first token; wall 67.6 s | 0 x 404; returns 0.28-0.37 s; wall 18.3 s |
| A,B,A,B... 3 rounds, ~4k-token histories | 4 x 404; returns 31.3-34.0 s; wall 193.8 s | 0 x 404; returns 0.35-0.48 s; wall 64.7 s (68.8 s in a second run) |
| 3 clients at once, 3 turns, ~0.6k tokens | 3 x 404; wall 30.3 s; 1.75 events/s | 0 x 404; wall 16.1 s (17.0 s); 3.30 events/s |
| 1 client, 5 turns, 3 conversations | follow-ups 0.25-0.36 s, 0 x 404 | follow-ups 0.25-0.35 s, 0 x 404 |
| 1 client, 128-token decode, 4 runs per group, groups alternated both ways | 39.5 tok/s mean over 4 groups | 38.7 tok/s mean over 4 groups (group means 35.9-40.8; the spread is run-order drift: the first order showed -3.5%, the reversed order -0.5%) |
Every answer of every turn was identical before and after (greedy, real model, all three conversation scenarios).
Cold resume = 0.35-0.48 s to first token at 4k tokens (copy back + the new tail) against 31-34 s to read the same history again.

## Memory accounting
- Cold session = 51 MiB (DeltaNet state) + 64 KiB per token (+4 B per token id): ~120 MiB at 1.1k tokens, ~310 MiB at 4k,
  ~570 MiB at 8k. 2 GiB therefore holds about 6 sessions of 4k tokens or all 16 of 1k.
- GPU: unchanged. One hot cache as before; process VRAM 4,504 MiB at idle-decode in both binaries; all-programs peaks in
  the scenarios 5.27-5.54 GB (before: 5.48-5.70 GB, the extra came from re-reads).
- Host: current RSS sampled every 0.5 s peaked at 1,038 MiB with one 4k cold session and 1,149 MiB with three small ones
  (load itself reaches ~870 MiB). No disk writes.

## Gates
A continuity: 0 x 404 in all three multi-client scenarios (before 3-6): PASS.
B cold resume <= 25% of a re-read: about 1-1.5%: PASS.
C single client: first token and follow-ups unchanged, decode within run-order noise (-0.5% to -3.5%, signs depend on order), VRAM same: PASS.
D 2-3 clients: aggregate events/s 1.75 -> 3.30, no 5xx: PASS.
E identical greedy output with and without demotion: real model 3/3 scenarios; tiny-model test across displaced conversations: PASS.
F bounds: constants + unit tests (count, bytes, oversize, replace, LRU); host RSS growth matches the formula: PASS.
G tests: store lifecycle (claim, 409/404, lease release, budget, oversize, replacement) and server_cold (displaced
conversations return unchanged and are not read again; unknown id 404; malformed 400; different history answers as a
new conversation; restart loses sessions). Removing the shelving made the main test fail (404), so it guards the feature.

## Edge cases and what is not covered
- Same-session contention: Store-level tests (claim twice -> Running); not driven end to end (a tiny model is too fast to
  overlap two requests deterministically). Eviction during generation cannot occur: a running session is in no list.
- Interrupted resume: a cancelled or failed turn after a thaw goes through the existing `recover`, which re-reads the
  previous history (one replay, as before for a hot session); the cold copy is not re-created. A thaw that cannot get GPU
  memory returns the cold session and answers 503; that path is covered only at Store level, not by a pressure test.
- Incompatible history: rebuilt and answers as a new conversation (tested). Oversized: not shelved, next request 404
  and the client sends its full history. Restart: all sessions lost by design (tested); nothing is persisted.
- Limits are constants, not flags. Engines other than Qwen3.5 CUDA keep one hot slot (a displaced session is dropped).

## Decision
GO. Independent clients no longer evict each other; a displaced conversation resumes in ~0.4 s instead of re-reading its
history (up to 30+ s at 4k tokens) with identical output, bounded host memory, unchanged GPU use and unchanged
single-client latency and throughput. Next justified milestone: none required by this evidence; the open items above
(a pressure test of a failed thaw, re-shelving after a cancelled resume, configurable limits) are small follow-ups.
